Build a GitHub Copilot Extension called prcheck. This is a GitHub Copilot Chat skill that team members invoke using @prcheck in GitHub Copilot Chat inside VS Code or GitHub.com. It performs deep domain-specific code review on top of what GitHub Copilot already does automatically.
PROJECT NAME: prcheck
EXTENSION NAME: @prcheck
REPO NAME: prcheck
PURPOSE: Deep AI-powered PR review skill for GitHub Copilot Chat — security, architecture, performance, bugs, and domain-specific rules for all languages
HOW IT WORKS:
Developer opens a PR on GitHub. GitHub Copilot already runs its standard review automatically. Developer then opens Copilot Chat and types @prcheck review this PR. The prcheck extension fetches the PR diff from GitHub API using the current repo context, runs deep multi-layer analysis, and streams back a structured review with severity levels and fix suggestions directly inside Copilot Chat.
TECH STACK:
Runtime: Node.js 20+ with TypeScript. GitHub Copilot Extensions SDK — @github/copilot-extensions for building the skill. GitHub API via Octokit REST client to fetch PR diff and file contents and metadata. Express.js for the extension server endpoint. GitHub App authentication for API access. Deployed as a simple Node.js server that GitHub Copilot Chat calls as an agent. Zod for input validation. dotenv for config.
EXTENSION COMMANDS — @prcheck supports these commands:
@prcheck review — Full deep review of current PR covering all categories.
@prcheck security — Security-only deep scan of the PR diff.
@prcheck performance — Performance and efficiency analysis only.
@prcheck architecture — Architecture, design patterns, coupling, cohesion analysis.
@prcheck bugs — Bug detection and logic error analysis only.
@prcheck summary — Executive summary of PR changes in plain English.
@prcheck fix [issue] — Suggest specific fix for a mentioned issue.
@prcheck help — Show all available commands with examples.
REVIEW CATEGORIES — for each PR diff analyze all of these:
Category 1 — Security Vulnerabilities:
SQL injection risks. XSS vulnerabilities. Hardcoded secrets, API keys, passwords, tokens. Insecure dependencies. Authentication and authorization bypasses. Sensitive data exposed in logs. OWASP Top 10 violations. Insecure deserialization. Path traversal risks. Race conditions in auth flows.
Category 2 — Code Quality and Best Practices:
SOLID principles violations. DRY violations — duplicated logic. Functions longer than 50 lines. Deeply nested code more than 4 levels. Magic numbers and magic strings without constants. Missing error handling. Poor naming conventions. Dead code and unreachable code. Missing or inadequate comments on complex logic. God classes and god functions.
Category 3 — Performance Issues:
N+1 query problems. Missing database indexes based on query patterns. Synchronous blocking operations that should be async. Unnecessary loops inside loops. Memory leaks — unclosed connections, event listeners not removed. Large payloads not paginated. Missing caching for expensive operations. String concatenation in loops instead of builders.
Category 4 — Bug Detection:
Null and undefined dereference without null checks. Off-by-one errors in loops. Integer overflow risks. Incorrect boolean logic. Missing edge case handling — empty arrays, zero values, negative numbers. Incorrect error propagation — errors swallowed silently. Race conditions in concurrent code. Wrong variable used in similar-looking assignments.
Category 5 — Architecture:
Business logic leaking into controllers or views. Direct database calls from wrong layers. Circular dependencies. Tight coupling between modules. Missing abstraction — hardcoded implementation instead of interface. Violation of separation of concerns. Missing dependency injection. Wrong use of design patterns.
LANGUAGE SUPPORT — detect language from file extension and apply language-specific rules:
Java: check for proper Spring annotations, transactional boundaries, thread safety, connection pool usage, checked exception handling.
Python: check for proper async await usage, type hints missing, mutable default arguments, global state.
TypeScript/JavaScript: check for proper Promise handling, missing await, type any usage, memory leaks in React hooks, missing dependency arrays.
Go: check for goroutine leaks, error handling patterns, proper defer usage.
SQL: check for missing WHERE clauses on updates, missing indexes, N+1 patterns.
Any other language: apply general best practices for security quality performance bugs architecture.
REVIEW OUTPUT FORMAT — stream back structured markdown:
Start with an executive summary: one paragraph describing what this PR does and overall risk level which is Critical or High or Medium or Low.
Then show findings grouped by severity:
CRITICAL — must fix before merge: each finding shows emoji fire, file name and line number, issue title, explanation of why it is dangerous, exact code snippet from the diff, suggested fix as code snippet.
HIGH — should fix before merge: each finding shows emoji warning, file name and line number, issue title, explanation, code snippet, suggested fix.
MEDIUM — consider fixing: each finding shows emoji yellow circle, issue title, explanation, suggestion.
LOW — nice to have: each finding shows emoji blue circle, issue title, quick tip.
End with a scorecard table showing: Security score out of 10, Quality score out of 10, Performance score out of 10, Overall score out of 10, Recommendation which is Approve or Request Changes or Block.
